# Repository Guidelines

## General Principles

- Keep new modules and code concise.
- Avoid over-engineering and oversized files.
- Flag files that should be refactored before expanding them further.
- Match the existing syntax, style, naming, and structure of the codebase.
- Look for obvious bugs before making or finalizing changes.
- Prefer concise, precise code and documentation changes.
- Review existing files before refactoring or changing behavior.
- For major changes, make a short to-do list and get user approval before implementation.
- If context becomes confusing, write notes to `/docs/activity-log.md`.
- Do not auto-commit activity logs or documentation-only notes unless explicitly asked.
- Markdown filenames must use kebab-case, for example `some-description-changes.md`.
- Comments must be one line and one sentence.
- Do not use emojis or decorative special characters in comments.

## Code Quality

- Choose appropriate data structures and algorithms for the problem.
- Follow least privilege and do not expose data unnecessarily.
- Do not add external libraries unless clearly necessary.
- Use the project dependency file for dependency versions.
- Avoid redundancy unless it improves usability or clarity.

## Version Control

- Commit after significant changes when asked to work through git.
- Keep commits focused and atomic.
- Use clear commit messages.
- Never push any branch unless explicitly asked.

## AI Restrictions

- Do not include customer personal data such as names, contacts, account numbers, or transactions unless the user explicitly approves an exemption.
- Do not include credentials such as passwords, API keys, tokens, or connection strings.
